// `map`'s functoriality stated as a law beside the definition it governs:
// mapping a composite is mapping twice (Bird, _An Introduction to the Theory
// of Lists_, 1987, § 2). The list `map` of `recursive-lists.gandr` takes its
// function as a value, and a rule names only its block's operations, so the
// law is stated over `map` presented as an operation with its function
// defunctionalised (Reynolds, _Definitional Interpreters for Higher-Order
// Programming Languages_, 1972): a function is a name `app` applies, and
// `compose` builds one from two. The coincidence check certifies the law with
// the definition's own rules, and the `normalizes` row fires it. The same law
// written over the `def` is refused in `../../pathological/data/map-law-on-a-def.gandr`.

/// Lists with `map` as an operation: `mapNil` and `mapCons` define it,
/// `appCompose` reads a composite, `composeAssoc` orients composition, and
/// `mapMap` is the law, mapping twice fused into mapping once.
@[ normalizes("map(inc, map(dbl, cons(one, nil))) ~> cons(app(inc, app(dbl, one)), nil)") ]
sign ListMap {
  sort E : Type;
  sort F : Type;
  sort L : Type;

  oper nil : L;
  oper cons : (x : E, xs : L) --> L;
  oper app : (f : F, x : E) --> E;
  oper compose : (f : F, g : F) --> F;
  oper map : (f : F, xs : L) --> L;
  oper inc : F;
  oper dbl : F;
  oper one : E;

  rule mapNil : map(f, nil()) <=> nil();
  rule mapCons : map(f, cons(x, xs)) <=> cons(app(f, x), map(f, xs));
  rule appCompose : app(compose(f, g), x) <=> app(f, app(g, x));
  rule composeAssoc : compose(compose(f, g), h) <=> compose(f, compose(g, h));
  rule mapMap : map(f, map(g, xs)) <=> map(compose(f, g), xs);
}
